.TH CCOL_DYNMQ_TRY_RECV_ZC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc \- try to receive from a dynamic queue with no block
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(ccol_dynamic_queue *dq,
                                 c_message_t *target_buf);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function tries to remove the message at the head of the dynamic queue
and to give it back in target_buf. If the queue is empty, the call returns
ccol_container_empty immediately; it never waits for a message.

On success, the ownership of the data pointer of the message moves to the
caller: target_buf->data is the pointer that the sender gave, and
target_buf->size is its size in bytes. The caller is the one that frees
the data after it finishes with it. The library copies no bytes; only
the pointer and its size move. Because a sender can put a message into
the queue with a NULL data pointer and a size of zero, and such a
message arrives in the same way, target_buf->data can legitimately be
NULL with target_buf->size at 0, so check it before you dereference it.

On every failure, the function does not change target_buf, and the queue
keeps everything that it held.

Use this receive call immediately after a callback from ccol_select(),
ccol_select_timed(), or an ccol_event_loop reports that a dynamic queue
selectable is ready. Those report readiness only and never take the
message themselves, so another consumer can take the message first;
ccol_container_empty covers exactly that case.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
dq          Dynamic queue to receive from
target_buf  Buffer that gets the message together with the ownership
            of the data.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success         - The function received a message. The caller owns
                       target_buf->data and must free it.
ccol_invalid_args    - dq or target_buf is NULL
ccol_container_empty - The queue is empty; the function does not change
                       target_buf.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function never blocks.
The function succeeds while sends on the queue are off, because turning
sends off only stops new messages from entering the queue; a receive
gets the messages that the queue already holds.
A receive that succeeds wakes one write-direction waiter on this queue,
which is a ccol_select() call, a ccol_select_timed() call, or an
ccol_event_loop registration.
The function is thread-safe.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
